course: dictionary
lesson: 11759
title: Baptised: a word from church and life
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from a special place, like a church. Baptised is one of them. It is about a ceremony, a special event with set words and actions. «I was baptised as a baby in this church.» The clues are church and baby: this happens to a person in a church.
explain: In that sentence, baptised is a verb, the past form of baptise, so it tells you something already happened. «Some people are baptised as adults, by full immersion in a river or pool.» The clue here is river or pool: the person goes fully under the water.
explain: Always read the words around it and test your meaning. If you swap in "washed with water as a sign of joining the Church" and the sentence still makes sense, your meaning fits. Baptised can also be an adjective, as in «a baptised Christian», meaning a person who has had baptism.
explain: Be careful with "baptised in" or "baptised by". These can mean having a hard first experience of something, not a church ceremony at all. The rest of the sentence shows which meaning is used.
fact: Baptism is a Christian ceremony. The person is washed with water as a sign of joining Christ and the Church.
fact: «Baptised» is the past form of the verb baptise.
fact: «Baptised» can also be an adjective, as in «a baptised Christian», meaning someone who has had baptism.
fact: «Baptised in» or «baptised by» can mean having a first hard experience of something.
fact: In American English the spelling is baptized, with a z.
exercise: «I was baptised as a baby in this church.» What does baptised mean here? | Given a prize for singing | *Given the Christian ceremony of baptism, washed with water | Given a new name at school | Taught to swim in a pool
exercise: Certificates record when and where you were baptised. What do certificates record? | How fast you can swim | The colour of the church door | *When and where you were baptised | Your favourite hymn
exercise: «Some people are baptised as adults, by full immersion in a river or pool.» What does full immersion mean? | Drinking a little water | *Going fully under the water | Sitting in a boat | Standing at the edge of the water
test: Which word in «a baptised Christian» is the adjective? | *baptised | a | person | Christian
test: «He was baptised in hard work on his first week at the farm.» What does baptised in mean here? | *Had a hard first experience of hard work | Was given a prize for good work | Was washed in the farm's water tank | Went swimming for fun
exam: Which sentence uses baptised as a church ceremony, not as a hard first experience? | *«I was baptised as a baby in this church.» | «He was baptised in hard work on his first day.» | «She was baptised by the cold rain on the walk.» | «They were baptised in the deep end of the job.»
